# Line endings are decided HERE, once, for every clone.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites every text
# file to CRLF on checkout. For a repository whose artifacts are Linux
# containers that is not a cosmetic difference, and the way it fails is
# genuinely hard to read:
#
#	exec /docker-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# The script is right there. What is missing is the INTERPRETER. A shebang line
# ending `#!/bin/sh\r` asks the kernel for a program literally named `/bin/sh\r`,
# there is no such file, and the error names the script rather than the thing it
# could not find. A bind-mounted script fails differently and no more clearly:
# every line ends in a carriage return, so `set -eu` becomes an illegal option
# and `nginx` becomes a command not found.
#
# `eol=lf` beats autocrlf: it is a per-path instruction that a client setting
# cannot override, so a Windows checkout of this repository gets LF whatever the
# machine is configured to do, and an editor that writes CRLF is normalised on
# commit.
#
# AFTER PULLING THIS FILE INTO AN EXISTING CLONE the working tree is still
# whatever it was checked out as. One command fixes it:
#
#	git add --renormalize . && git status
#
# The build no longer depends on this being right - Dockerfiles strip carriage
# returns from anything they copy, and the bind-mounted entrypoints are stripped
# at container start - but the repository should be correct at rest as well as
# survivable.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out for the ones that are executed rather than read. Nothing here
# behaves differently to the rule above; they are listed so that a change to
# that line cannot silently take these with it.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.mjs     text eol=lf
*.bash    text eol=lf
Dockerfile*  text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.conf    text eol=lf
*.sql     text eol=lf

# NEVER touched. A file Git guesses wrong about and "normalises" is a corrupt
# file, and the guess is made per blob rather than per extension.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
*.ttf     binary
*.zip     binary
*.gz      binary
*.tgz     binary
